```rust
/// Check whether `text` contains `word` as a whole word (case-insensitive).
fn contains_word(text: &str, word: &str) -> bool {
    if text.is_empty() || word.is_empty() {
        return false;
    }
    let lower = text.to_lowercase();
    let mut start = 0;
    while let Some(rel) = lower[start..].find(word) {
        let abs = start + rel;
        let before_ok = abs == 0 || !lower.as_bytes()[abs - 1].is_ascii_alphanumeric();
        let after_end = abs + word.len();
        let after_ok = after_end >= lower.len() || !lower.as_bytes()[after_end].is_ascii_alphanumeric();
        if before_ok && after_ok {
            return true;
        }
        start = abs + word.len();
    }
    false
}
```

Wait, `find` on a `&str` slice — byte index returned. That's fine since `find` returns a valid char boundary. The check `lower.as_bytes()[abs - 1]` — abs > 0 so abs-1 is valid. `after_end` is abs + word.len() — if word is ASCII (all shelved labels are ASCII), this is fine.

Hmm, but this `contains_word` — for "won't fix", the word "won't" — the apostrophe: is `'` alphanumeric? No. So "won't" matching within "won't fix" — before_ok: char before 'w' is start or non-alnum. after_end at the apostrophe... wait "won't" — the token is "won't" with apostrophe inside. `find("won't")` in "won't fix" — matches at 0, after_end is 5, next char is ' ' (non-alnum) → after_ok. So "won't" matches. Good.

For "fix": `find("fix")` in "won't fix" matches at 6, before char at 5 is ' ' → before_ok. after_end at 9 = end → after_ok. So "fix" would match as whole word if "fix" were in the list. Fine.

