/// Check whether `text` contains `word` as a whole word (case-insensitive),
/// where a "whole word" is bounded by non-alphanumeric characters or string
/// edges. This is what prevents "collateral"/"bilateral" from false-positively
/// matching the "later" shelved label via plain substring.
fn contains_word(text: &str, word: &str) -> bool {
    if text.is_empty() || word.is_empty() {
        return false;
    }
    let lower = text.to_lowercase();
    let mut start = 0;
    while let Some(rel) = lower[start..].find(word) {
        let abs = start + rel;
        let before_ok = abs == 0 || !lower.as_bytes()[abs - 1].is_ascii_alphanumeric();
        let after = abs + word.len();
        let after_ok = after >= lower.len() || !lower.as_bytes()[after].is_ascii_alphanumeric();
        if before_ok && after_ok {
            return true;
        }
        start = abs + word.len();
    }
    false
}
```

Wait — `find` returns byte offset relative to the slice. `lower[start..]` — start must be a char boundary. `abs + word.len()` is a char boundary because find returns matches at char boundaries and word is ASCII... Actually `abs` is a char boundary in `lower` (find guarantees match start is a char boundary). But what if `word` is non-ASCII? All shelved labels are ASCII, so fine. And `start = abs + word.len()` — if word is ASCII, abs+len is a char boundary. All SHELVED_LABELS are ASCII. Good.

